The molt's shed ledger — design #533 D2 and S2. One line per shed passage: what
it said, the NAMED CLASS that licensed shedding it, and where the survivor went.

    <path> — class: <class>; survivor: <where it went, or none>

The five classes are #533 D4, and naming one is not a formality: the class is a
schema field on every survey subagent's structured return, so an agent that
cannot name a class cannot emit the shed at all. `live constraint`,
`rejected alternative`, `open hole`, `measurement` and `world fact` are the
classes that SURVIVE; a shed line names the class it was weighed against and
found not to be, and `saga` is the residue.

THIS IS A SCRATCH SLATE, NOT A REGISTRY — the same contract as `.chug/doc-reread`
and for the same reason. `.chug/tasks/check-molt.sh` reads the lines your diff
ADDS against its base, never this file's contents, so every line below is inert
the moment it merges and licenses nothing on any later branch. A rebase cannot
destroy a real assertion, and a merged line cannot become a standing waiver.
Nothing prunes it and nothing has to.

There is no archive. Git holds the history and the job records hold the work, so
the sheddings themselves are deleted rather than filed: a tracked archive is
corpus the next molt must molt, and an untracked one is out of every gate's and
job's reach. The absence of a third option is #533 D2's whole argument, and this
ledger is the accounting, not the archive.

